    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although it has long been known that major histocompatibility complex (MHC) markers play an important role in the adaptive immune system, recent work has implicated MHC Class I in neuronal plasticity in the central nervous system (CNS).  However, little is known about the consequences of the innate immune response on neuronal MHC I expression and its subsequent effect on plasticity, an aspect critical to stem cell-derived neurons and their use in transplantation therapy.  In this study, we generate neurons from induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) derived from wild type and MHC I – deficient mice to use as a knockout model.  We then utilize the inflammatory cytokine interferon-gamma (IFNγ) as a model of the innate immune response to induce MHC I expression in these neurons.  We observe that wild type neurons exhibit an increase in MHC I, whereas the knockout-derived neurons display no changes in MHC I levels as predicted.  The iPSCs and resulting neurons produced were further characterized for use in future connectivity and transplantation studies to probe the effects of MHC I on stem cell transplant survival and incorporation, with the final goal of employing non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) to attenuate the inflammatory cytokine effect and increase transplant efficacy.  These results provide important insight into the relationship between the innate immune response and stem cell-derived neuronal integration, taking a crucial step toward the future goal of using patient-specific iPSCs in clinical transplant therapies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
